[[!meta title="Shutting down Tails"]]

There are 2 ways of shutting down Tails.

[[!toc startlevel=2]]

With both techniques, all the data stored in [[!wikipedia Random-access_memory
desc="RAM"]] is securely erased to protect you from [[cold boot
attacks|doc/advanced_topics/cold_boot_attacks]].

Standard shutdown from the system menu
--------------------------------------

The standard shutdown procedure is slower, but prevents damage to your
Persistent Storage and data loss.

1. Open the system menu in the upper-right corner of the screen.

   [[!img desktop/system-with-wifi-only.png class="screenshot" alt=""]]

1. Select [[!img lib/system-shutdown.png link="no" class="symbolic" alt="Power Off"]].

1. Select **Power Off**.

1. A **Power Off** confirmation dialog appears.

   [[!img power_off_without_inhibitor.png class="screenshot" alt=""]]

   If an application needs to be closed or an open document needs to be saved
   before shutting down, the dialog includes some details.

   [[!img power_off_with_inhibitor.png class="screenshot" alt=""]]

1. Select the **Power Off** button to confirm.

   Even without confirming or saving the open documents, Tails will shut down
   after 60 seconds.

Emergency shutdown
------------------

To trigger an emergency shutdown, unplug the USB stick or eject the DVD from
which Tails is running. Doing so might be faster than shutting down from the
system menu.

<div class="caution">

<p>You risk breaking your Persistent Storage and losing data when you unplug
your USB stick while Tails is running.</p>

<p>If you use a Persistent Storage, only unplug your Tails USB stick in an
emergency.</p>

</div>
